" (https://www.reformparty.uk/policies/economic-rescue-plan/download)\nciteturn24view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 36; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.reformparty.uk/policies/economic-rescue-plan/download\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 836\nL0@P2: ECONOMIC\nL1@P2-3: RESCUE PLANForeword by Robert Jenrick MP\nL2@P3: Britain’s public finances are in a parlous state. Mismanagement and cowardice by successive\nL3@P3: Chancellors have left us £3 trillion in debt, with soaring inflation, and a collapse in consumer\nL4@P3: confidence. Monthly borrowing costs have reached record levels. Meanwhile, the tax burden\nL5@P3: is at its highest level since the 1940s.\nL6@P3: This happened for one reason: Westminster is addicted to spending – but not on the services\nL7@P3: that matter to the British people. Under George Osborne’s supposed “austerity”, foreign aid\nL8@P3: spending expanded dramatically, while military spending was frozen. Andy Burnham has\nL9@P3: already overseen the early release of thousands of prisoners, due to mass immigration and a\nL10@P3: simultaneous failure to build prisons, while his Foreign Secretary pledges almost £90 million\nL11@P3: to Gaza. Just last year, taxpayers spent £12 billion, or £414 per household, on benefits for\nL12@P3: foreign nationals.\nL13@P3: John Healey has a chance to break from decades of failed consensus. To gain the confidence\nL14@P3: of voters, he will need to deliver meaningful tax relief for ordinary people. To earn the\nL15@P3: confidence of bond markets, and in turn to lower borrowing costs and return money to\nL16@P3: people’s pockets, he will need to keep control of borrowing. To do both, he must meaningfully\nL17@P3: reduce spending, including on welfare. And do so now.\nL18@P3: The odds are not in his favour. Just like Rachel Reeves, he is trapped by a Parliamentary\nL19@P3: Labour Party unwilling to countenance meaningful spending cuts, especially to their beloved\nL20@P3: benefits budget.\nL21@P3: Unsurprisingly, everything reported so far suggests John Healey will dodge the tough\nL22@P3: decisions needed to put our economy back on solid footing. A ‘mañana budget’ would be a\nL23@P3: cowardly abdication of his duty to safeguard the country’s finances.\nL24@P3: We can’t continue like this. The economy needs a rescue plan. Working people need a lifeline.\nL25@P3: This Economic Rescue Plan represents Reform’s alternative proposals. Unlike the other parties,\nL26@P3: we are unwilling to allow Britain to go gently into the good night of managed decline. We\nL27@P3: are unwilling to allow the working people of this country to be immiserated. Therefore, we\nL28@P3: present this coherent, fully-costed plan to deliver substantial tax relief and debt reduction,\nL29@P3: rescuing both the Exchequer and household budgets.\nL30@P3: Reform won’t flinch from taking the decisions needed to put us on a better footing. We will\nL31@P3: confront out-of-control spending straight away - slashing the ballooning benefits bill, cutting\nL32@P3: foreign aid and ending net zero subsidies - so we can give households £1,300 in tax relief.\nL33@P3: Our proposals are accompanied by more than 60 individual job-creating measures that will\nL34@P3: free up the economy. That means bringing down the tax burden, bringing down inflation, and\nL35@P3: bringing down interest rates. The result will be a vibrant, growing private sector that delivers\nL36@P3: well-paid jobs for British citizens.\nL37@P3: Britain is broke. Britain needs Reform.\nL38@P3: Rt. Hon. Robert Jenrick MP\nL39@P3: Reform UK’s Shadow Chancellor\nL40@P3-4: 4 ECONOMIC RESCUE PLANCONTENTS\nL41@P4: Foreword by Robert Jenrick MP\nL42@P4: Introduction\nL43@P4: The Situation\nL44@P4: Our Choices\nL45@P4: » Labour’s Choices\nL46@P4: » The Conservatives’ Choices\nL47@P4: Cutting Your Taxes\nL48@P4: » Increasing the Tax-Free Personal Allowance to £15k\nL49@P4: » Cutting Energy Bills by £250\nL50@P4: » Abolishing the Energy Profits Levy\nL51@P4: » Increasing the VAT Threshold to £150k\nL52@P4: » Making the 5p Cut in Fuel Duty Permanent\nL53@P4: » Scrap the Jobs Tax and Tax Migrant Workers Instead\nL54@P4: » No Tax on Overtime or Reservist Pay\nL55@P4: » Saving Our Pubs\nL56@P4: » Scrapping the Death Tax on Family Farms and Family Business\nL57@P4: Spending That Matters to You\nL58@P4: » Additional Expenditure\nL59@P4: » Making Welfare Work\nL60@P4: » Scrapping Net Zero Subsidies\nL61@P4: » Cutting Foreign Aid\nL62@P4: » Shrinking QUANGOs and Civil Service\nL63@P4: » Stop Funding Dead-End Degrees\nL64@P4: » Savings Summary\nL65@P4: Growing the Pie\nL66@P4: » Unlocking the North Sea\nL67@P4: » Reindustrialising Britain\nL68@P4: » Getting Homes Built in Our Cities\nL69@P4: » Building Critical National Infrastructure\nL70@P4: » Joining the AI Revolution\nL71@P4: » Reducing the Cost of Hiring British Workers\nL72@P4: » Unleashing the City to Invest in the Real Economy\nL73@P4: 4\nL74@P4: 7\nL75@P4: 9\nL76@P4: 11\nL77@P4: 12\nL78@P4: 12\nL79@P4: 13\nL80@P4: 13\nL81@P4: 14\nL82@P4: 15\nL83@P4: 15\nL84@P4: 15\nL85@P4: 15\nL86@P4: 16\nL87@P4: 16\nL88@P4: 16\nL89@P4: 18\nL90@P4: 18\nL91@P4: 18\nL92@P4: 19\nL93@P4: 19\nL94@P4: 20\nL95@P4: 21\nL96@P4: 22\nL97@P4: 23\nL98@P4: 23\nL99@P4: 24\nL100@P4: 25\nL101@P4: 26\nL102@P4: 26\nL103@P4: 26\nL104@P4: 27\nL105@P4-5: ECONOMIC RESCUE PLAN 5CONTENTS » Getting Motorists Moving\nL106@P5: » Using Brexit Freedoms\nL107@P5: » General Deregulatory Measures\nL108@P5: Indicative Fiscal Plan\nL109@P5: » Fiscal Rules\nL110@P5: Conclusion\nL111@P5: Endnotes\nL112@P5: 28\nL113@P5: 28\nL114@P5: 28\nL115@P5: 30\nL116@P5: 30\nL117@P5: 32\nL118@P5: 33\nL119@P5-6: 6 ECONOMIC RESCUE PLANIntroduction\nL120@P6: For most working people, the cost of living is a daily reality and a daily worry. Diesel is £2.00\nL121@P6: per litre, a homemade ham and cheese sandwich costs 30-40% more than five years ago1, a\nL122@P6: 500g pack of beef mince from the supermarket will now set you back over £3,2 and a Freddo\nL123@P6: - having already been subject to shrinkflation - retails for up to 45p.3 The average pub pint,\nL124@P6: meanwhile, has risen from under £4.00 to around £5.20.4\nL125@P6: Yet for many politicians, the cost of living is little more than a buzzword, something to which\nL126@P6: they pay lip service in TV studios. Instead of doing things to help cut the cost of living,\nL127@P6: like cutting taxes on working people and family businesses, and paying for this by cutting\nL128@P6: wasteful spending, they keep doing the opposite. Now even the triple lock - a cost-of-living\nL129@P6: protection for pensioners who have worked hard and paid in all their lives - is under threat\nL130@P6: from Andy Burnham.\nL131@P6: When prices and taxes go up, establishment politicians like to point the finger at factors\nL132@P6: beyond Britain’s control, such as Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 and the crisis in the\nL133@P6: Strait of Hormuz which began earlier this year. That way everyone in Westminster can carry\nL134@P6: on as before. No course correction is needed because no course correction is possible.\nL135@P6: In reality, Britain is so exposed to international crises because of the policies pursued by\nL136@P6: Conservative and Labour governments, not just over the last few years, but over the last\nL137@P6: three decades.\nL138@P6: Low-skilled mass migration has been used to artificially inflate GDP, paper over the structural\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Wealth of Our Nation: Restore Britain’s Economic Philosophy.docx (https://assets.nationbuilder.com/restorebritain/pages/1255/attachments/original/1785930638/The_Wealth_of_Our_Nation__Restore_Britain%E2%80%99s_Economic_Philosophy.pdf?1785930638=)\nciteturn24view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 64; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://assets.nationbuilder.com/restorebritain/pages/1255/attachments/original/1785930638/The_Wealth_of_Our_Nation__Restore_Britain%E2%80%99s_Economic_Philosophy.pdf?1785930638=\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 1252\nL0@P2: Contents\nL1@P2: Contents...............................................................................................................1\nL2@P2: Foreword.............................................................................................................. 2\nL3@P2: Executive Summary.............................................................................................. 4\nL4@P2: Introduction.........................................................................................................8\nL5@P2: Our Economic Principles.....................................................................................13\nL6@P2: Conclusion..........................................................................................................61\nL7@P2-3: 1Foreword\nL8@P3: The British state has now definitively become the enemy of the British people. Rather\nL9@P3: than serve our interests, an out-of-touch elite, addicted to central planning, has\nL10@P3: instead treated us like guinea pigs in some perverse experiment. They have waged an\nL11@P3: all-out war on our freedoms, now escalated by an incompetent Labour government\nL12@P3: and a bloated civil service.\nL13@P3: Businesses are allowed to stay in private hands, but increasingly find themselves\nL14@P3: fettered by regulation, price controls, minimum wages, employment laws, and a\nL15@P3: network of rapacious lawyers, increasingly feasting like parasites on the back of\nL16@P3: productive Britain. It is best described as a corporatist command economy – a kind of\nL17@P3: UK version of the USSR, which maintains the illusion of a free society but, short of\nL18@P3: dramatic change, will end in the same ignominious collapse. State-backed projects\nL19@P3: such as Net Zero and forced diversity have not only licensed far-reaching intrusion\nL20@P3: into our lives, but made us poorer. Our most successful rainmakers are leaving the\nL21@P3: country, to be replaced by low-grade immigrants who stand to benefit from our\nL22@P3: undiscerning, ever-expanding welfare state.\nL23@P3: This paper sets out the roadmap to restoring Britain as a competitive, free, and\nL24@P3: prosperous nation. We call for an end to state-led micromanagement. Such\nL25@P3: invasiveness undermines natural human relationships and distorts organic price\nL26@P3: mechanisms – both of which are needed for a society to flourish. We believe in\nL27@P3: government with a light touch. That means sound money, private property rights, and\nL28@P3: a small, judicious, patriotic state committed to serving, not smothering, the British\nL29@P3: people. We plot a responsible course of tax cuts in step with a reduction in state\nL30@P3: expenditure.\nL31@P3: Concerns may be raised about the scale of our ambitions, but we trust in the ingenuity\nL32@P3: of the British people. Enterprising Britons who have been forced to leave by the\nL33@P3: senselessness of our current system will have every reason to return while their\nL34@P3: replacements here on welfare will have every reason to leave.\nL35@P3: The spirit of achievement through hard work and honest dealing will once again be\nL36@P3: celebrated.\nL37@P3: Rupert Lowe MP\nL38@P3-4: 2“The love of money,” we know, “is the root of all evil;” but not the thing itself. The\nL39@P4: fault does not lie in the money, but in them that use it.\nL40@P4: John Wesley (“The Use of Money”, 1760)\nL41@P4: [T]he more the state “plans” the more difficult planning becomes for the individual.\nL42@P4: Friedrich von Hayek (The Road to Serfdom, 1944)\nL43@P4-5: 3ExecutiveSummary\nL44@P5: At Restore Britain, we diagnose the United Kingdom as experiencing sustained\nL45@P5: economic decline driven by deliberate policy choices made by all governing\nL46@P5: parties in recent decades.\nL47@P5: We note that Britain has shifted from a relatively low-tax, moderate-spending\nL48@P5: economy with strong public finances to one in which the state accounts for\nL49@P5: nearly half of economic activity, taxation reaches its highest share of national\nL50@P5: income since 1948, public debt has risen to nearly 100% of GDP, and GDP per\nL51@P5: head growth has stagnated at levels unseen since the Industrial Revolution.\nL52@P5: We highlight the continual flight of skilled British nationals abroad, combined\nL53@P5: with an even more damaging process of mass low-skilled immigration at home.\nL54@P5: To make matters worse, the British wealth creators who remain face excessive\nL55@P5: regulation, complex and high taxation, and deteriorating public service\nL56@P5: outcomes despite record spending. We reject the proposition that public services\nL57@P5: fail primarily due to underfunding. In our assessment, the growth in the size and\nL58@P5: scope of the state has crowded out the private sector, undermined\nL59@P5: competitiveness, and produced counter-productive results.\nL60@P5: We therefore propose a multi-decade project that seeks to wean the state off\nL61@P5: excessive spending while prioritising fiscal and monetary stability. We recognise\nL62@P5: the private sector as the primary engine of wealth creation and would seek to\nL63@P5: create legislative conditions to stimulate rapid economic growth. This would be\nL64@P5: supported by policies that strengthen the family and wider communities, uphold\nL65@P5: the rule of law, mark a return to modest and sensible regulation, and return\nL66@P5: decision-making power to the individual. We hold that prosperity arises from\nL67@P5: the hard work of the British people, not from the expansion of the state.\nL68@P5: MonetaryPolicy\nL69@P5: We criticise the Bank of England’s post-1997 independence and subsequent\nL70@P5: record, particularly the Quantitative Easing programmes that fuelled asset\nL71@P5: inflation, intergenerational unfairness in housing, and the post-COVID\nL72@P5-6: 4inflation peak of 11.1%. While maintaining our unequivocal commitment in price\nL73@P6: stability as currently defined our proposals include nominating new members of\nL74@P6: the Monetary Policy Committee who are committed to stability; requiring\nL75@P6: explicit authorisation from the Chancellor of the Exchequer for any monetary\nL76@P6: expansion, thereby ending the routine monetisation of deficits; placing greater\nL77@P6: interim emphasis on broad money-supply aggregates.\nL78@P6: FiscalFramework\nL79@P6: Fiscal stability constitutes the cornerstone of our approach: balancing the\n"
